## 1. Introduction
With the explosive expansion of electronic products, the commonly used lithium-ion batteries can hardly match the increased demand. This has led to the higher requirements of energy storage devices [1–3]. In this regard, lithium-based batteries with high energy density have attracted great attention due to the superior performances of lithium metal such as its high specific capacity $(3860\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}.\$ ) and lowest redox potential (-3.04 V) [4–6]. However, the uncontrollable growth of lithium dendrites may lead to their capacity fading and even safety hazards when they are coupled with flammable liquid organic solvent of electrolytes. This significantly limits the practical applications of lithium based batteries [7–9].
To solve the above problems, properties of electrolytes and the interface of anode/electrolyte have been regarded as two critical factors. A lot of efforts have been made in promoting the stabilities of anodes and electrolytes, such as concentrated electrolytes with flame-retardant [10] and introducing various electrolyte systems [11,12]. Among these efforts, the employment of solid state electrolytes has been regarded as a potential approach due to their less reactions with lithium anode and flammable resistance. The common solid state electrolytes could be classified into ceramicbased electrolytes (CEs) and polymer-based electrolytes (PEs). Though CEs have high Li ion conductivity and rigidity, their poor bending strength could easily lead to micro cracks which create paths for the growth of lithium dendrites, and the big interface resistance hampers the transportation of Li ion, reducing the stability and electrochemical performances of lithium based batteries [13–16]. Compared to CEs, PEs are not only conveniently processed, but are also highly flexible and provide satisfactory contact between lithium anode and electrolytes [17–19], making them more and more attractive. However, due to the slow segmental motion of polymer base, the transportation of Li ion in PEs is highly limited. Also, PEs exhibits poor physical properties including low mechanical strength and thermal stability [20–22], limiting their further developments.
In recent years, numerous studies have been done to increase the ionic conductivity and physical properties of PEs, such as the mixture of multiple polymer species [23–25], molecular modification of polymers [26] and introduction of additives [27–30]. Among these approaches, composite polymer electrolytes (CPEs) developed by incorporating polymers and inorganic additives were proved efficient in improving the ionic conductivity and physical properties of PEs. For example, nanomaterials were demonstrated useful in reinforcing the ionic conductivity, mechanical strength and thermostability of ether-based PEs by virtue of the interaction between electronegative functional groups, Lewis acid sites and polymer chains [31–33]. Recently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and MXene layers have been widely employed in the field of solid state electrolytes. These introduced MOFs could promote the conductivity of lithium ion and homogenize Li ion flux due to their large specific surface area, abundant active sites and adjustable composition and structure [34–38]. And, MXene layers could enhance the electrical and mechanical properties of CPEs due to their fascinating physical and chemical characteristics [39–42]. Furthermore, MOFs and MXene exhibited outstanding flame resistant performance. However, MOFs nanoparticles and MXene layers still face the problem of agglomeration and re-stacking [34,43]. This limits their efficient exposure to polymers, attenuating their enhancement performances on CPEs. To solve the above problem, developing hybrid nanosheets with MOFs extended horizontally on the MXene skeleton is a viable approach. It could improve the dispersion of additives as well as produce synergistic enhancement effects on CPEs. Therefore, the ordered combination of MOFs and MXene is promising for improving the performances of CPEs.
Herein, two-dimensional ZIF-8 modified $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ -MXene (ZIF8@MXene) nanosheets were fabricated via the in-situ growth of ZIF-8 on MXene layers, and a polymer composite electrolyte (PEZIF- $8@$ MXene) consisting of poly (ethylene oxide) (PEO), copolymer poly (vinylidene fluoride-hexafluoro propylene) (PVDF-HFP) base. Also, a homogenously distributed ZIF- $8@$ MXene nanosheet was fabricated. In this electrolyte, ZIF- $8@$ MXene added has four advantages: Firstly, ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene nanosheets hydrogen bonded with polymer molecules to enhance the tensile strength of the electrolyte. Secondly, ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene nanosheets exhibited abundant functional groups and Lewis acid sites which increased the dissociation of lithium salts, thereby increasing the lithium ion conductivity. Thirdly, the large specific surface area and pores of ZIF- $8@$ MXene nanosheets provided channels for Li ion transport. Last but not the least, the high heat-resistant quality of ZIF$8@$ MXene nanosheets improved the thermostability and flame resistance of PE-ZIF- ${\boldsymbol{8}}\varpi$ MXene. In consequence, the developed flame resistant electrolyte exhibited high ionic conductivity (4.4 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ), high ionic transfer number (0.76) and an enhanced tensile strength $(3.77\mathrm{\:MPa}),$ . In addition, lithium dendrites were efficiently restrained by PE-ZIF- $\cdot8@$ MXene. As a result, the lithium symmetric batteries assembled with PE-ZIF- $8@$ MXene exhibited superior stabilities with a long cycle life up to $2000\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the resulted Li|PE-ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\alpha}}$ MXene|LiFe ${\mathrm{PO}}_{4}$ batteries displayed good cycling stability with a capacity retention of $89.6\%$ after 500 cycles.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of materials
Preparation of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene layers: $3.2\ {\tt g}$ LiF was dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ HCl solution (9 M). $_\mathrm{~1~g~}$ TiAlC MAX phase was added into the solution under stirring. After stirring the solution for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature, the mixture solution was centrifugated and washed with deionized water, and the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene layers were collected (about $1\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}}$ concentration).
Preparation of ZIF-8 modified $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene nanosheets (ZIF8@MXene): $0.175\textrm{g Z n}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dispersed in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ MXene dispersion as solution A. $0.527\mathrm{~g~}$ 2-methylimidazole was dispersed in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ methyl alcohol as solution B. Then, solution B was slowly added into solution A under stirring. After stirring for another $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the mixed solution was washed repeatedly with deionized water and concentrated. The ZIF- ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}8@{\overline{{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}}}$ MXene nanosheets were obtained after freeze drying.
Preparation of polymer electrolytes: $_{\mathrm{~1~g~}}$ of copolymer poly (vinylidene fluoride-hexafluoro propylene) (PVDF-HFP) was dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ acetone at $40\ \mathrm{^\circC}$ and then $_{0.05\mathrm{~g~}}$ poly(ethylene oxide) (PEO) was added into the solution under stirring. The mixture was stirred at $55\ \mathrm{^\circC}$ for another $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then, the solution was blade coated onto glass and dried to obtain the polymer base. It was infiltrated into liquid electrolytes $(1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ LiTFSI in DME: $\mathrm{DOL}\:=\:1{:}1\ \mathrm{vol\%}$ with $0.1\mathrm{~M~LiNO}_{3}$ and $1.0\mathrm{~M~LiPF}_{6}$ in EC: DMC: $\mathrm{EMC}=1{:}1{:}1{:}1\ \mathrm{vol\%}$ with $1.0\%$ VC) for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ and its surface was wiped dry. This resulted in the formation of pure polymer electrolytes (PE). The preparation process of polymer electrolytes with ZIF$8@$ MXene (PE-ZIF- $8@$ MXene) and PE-1 wt% ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ MXene was similar to that of PE except that $30~\mathrm{mg}$ and 10 mg ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene were dispersed in the initial acetone, respectively. Polymer electrolytes with $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene layers and ZIF-8 nanoparticles (PEMXene-ZIF-8) were prepared through similar process, the only difference being that $35~\mathrm{mg}$ MXene layers and $15~\mathrm{mg}$ ZIF-8 nanoparticles were added in the initial acetone.
## 2.2. Materials characterizations
The morphologies and elemental distribution of the samples were observed by a scanning electron microscope (SEM, Phenom LE, Phenom-World), attached X-ray energy spectrometer (EDS) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL, JEM-2100F). The Fourier transform infrared spectrometry (FTIR, Nicolet IS50, Thermo Fisher Scientific) and X-ray diffraction (XRD, PANalytical B.V., Netherlands) were conducted to measure the composition of the samples. The nitrogen adsorption/desorption tests were performed through a Micromeritics ASAP 2460 apparatus. The tensile strength of the samples was examined through a universal material testing machine (LR5KPlus Lloyd, UK) and the thermal stability of the samples was measured by a thermoelectric thermostat drying box.
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurements
To investigate the electrochemical performances of the prepared electrolytes, symmetric batteries with Li as electrodes and polymer electrolytes (PE, PE-ZIF- $8@$ MXene, PE-1 wt% ZIF$8@$ MXene and PE-MXene-ZIF-8 with LiTFSI) were assembled, and full batteries with Li anodes, LiFe $\mathsf{P O}_{4}$ cathodes and separators (PE and PE-ZIF- $\mathtt{8@}$ MXene with $\operatorname{LiPF}_{6}$ ) were also assembled. $\mathsf{L i F e P O}_{4}$ cathodes were fabricated by blading coated slurry (70 wt% LiFe $\mathsf{P O}_{4}$ : $20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ carbon black: $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ PVDF) on Al foil. To investigate the cycling stability of the electrolytes, electrochemical impedance spectrum (EIS), cyclic voltammetry (Chenghua, Shanghai), galvanostatic and long-term cycling tests (CT-4008 T, NEWARE, Shenzhen) were conducted with the assembled symmetric and full batteries. To evaluate the stable voltage range of the electrolytes, linear sweep voltammetry tests were conducted with symmetric batteries consisting of stainless-steel electrodes and polymer electrolytes (PE, PE-ZIF- $\cdot8@$ MXene and PE-MXene-ZIF-8 with LiTFSI). To evaluate the ionic conductivity of the polymer electrolytes, galvanostatic polarization and EIS were performed with stainless steel-based symmetric batteries. The ionic conductivities $(\upsigma)$ of the electrolytes were calculated using equation (1), where Rb is the bulk resistance of the electrolytes, S is the working area and L is the thickness of the electrolytes.
$$
\sigma=\frac{L}{R_{b}\times S}
$$
The $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ transference number $\left(\mathrm{{t}_{{L i+}}}\right)$ was estimated using equation (2), where $\triangle\mathsf{V}$ is the applied voltage, $\mathrm{I}_{0}$ and ${\bf R}_{0}$ are the current and interface resistance before polarization, respectively, and Iss and
Rss are the steady-state current and steady-state resistance after polarization, respectively.
$$
t_{L i^{+}}=\frac{I_{s s}(\Delta V-I_{0}R_{0})}{I_{0}(\Delta V-I_{s s}R_{s})}
$$
## 3. Results and discussion
The fabrication process of ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene nanosheets and PEZIF- $8@$ MXene is illustrated in Fig. 1. Owing to the abundant functional groups of ZIF-8@MXene nanosheets and the polymer base consisting of poly(ethylene oxide) (PEO) and copolymer poly (vinylidene fluoride-hexafluoro propylene) (PVDF-HFP), hydrogen bonds were generated between the nanosheets and polymer base, as obtained in other reports [43,44]. These were also confirmed by the FTIR spectra, as shown in Fig. S1. The peak of PE-ZIF-8@MXene belonging to hydroxyl group generated redshift compared to that of ZIF-8@MXene and PE; and the peaks belonging to fluorinecontaining groups and the CAN bond of ZIF- $\cdot8@$ MXene and PE also generated redshift after their combination, demonstrating the hydrogen-bonds between ZIF- $8@$ MXene and polymer molecules (Fig. 1b). Moreover, under the electric field condition, the fluorine-containing functional groups of MXene and PVDF-HFP and Lewis acid type zinc sites of ZIF-8 increased the dissociation of lithium salts. This was demonstrated by the blueshift of peaks belonging to $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left.-\mathrm{CF}_{2}\aftergroup\egroup\right.$ - and $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ bonds and the redshift of $_{C-0-C}$ peak in the presence of lithium salts (Fig. S2). Furthermore, the porous ZIF-8 and the interface between ZIF- $\cdot8@$ MXene nanosheets and polymers provided abundant transport paths for Li ion [45,46], accelerating Li ion transportation in the electrolytes (Fig. 1c). To reveal the morphologies and structure of ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene nanosheets, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and BrunnerEmmet-Teller (BET) measurements were performed.
As shown in Fig. 2a, 2D ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene nanosheets were clearly observed, and in-situ grown ZIF-8 nanoparticles were distributed along both sides of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene layers, preventing the stacking of ZIF- $8@$ MXene nanosheets. As revealed by the BET results (Fig. 2b and c), the ZIF-8@MXene nanosheets have a high specific surface area of $733.66\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m~}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}}$ and pore diameters of around $3.7~\mathrm{nm}$ , which are much higher than the specific surface area of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene reported [43]. This indicates the efficient combination of ZIF-8 and MXene nanosheets. Owing to the unique and uniform structure of ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene nanosheets, the morphologies of the polymer electrolyte with ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene (PE-ZIF- $\upmu$ MXene) were different from those of PE. As shown in Fig. 2d and S4, PE with a white color has many pores on its surface due to the liner structure of the polymers. On the contrary, PE-ZIF- $\cdot8@$ MXene has a uniform violet black and a tight surface without any visible pores (Fig. 2e). This can be ascribed to the abundant functional groups and unique structure of ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ MXene nanosheets. The relatively rigid ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene nanosheets chemically bonded with the polymer base, which prevented their agglomeration and led to their homogenous distribution in the polymer base (Fig. S4 and S5). It helped to reshape the structure resulting in a tight and uniform matrix. Therefore, when both flexible $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene layers and ZIF-8 nanoparticles were added into the polymer base, the agglomerated additives were distributed along the pores (Fig. S6). The uniform distribution of ZIF- $8@$ MXene nanosheets in the polymer electrolyte was further confirmed by the homogenous distribution of C, Zn, Ti elementals as revealed by the energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) results (Fig. S7). The $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) results displayed the chemical stability of ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ MXene nanosheets in the CPE (Fig. 2f).
To further investigate the conductivity and physical properties of PE-ZIF- $\cdot8@$ MXene and PE, electrochemical tests, thermal resistance and burning tests were conducted. As shown in Fig. 3a-b, PE-ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene exhibited superior Li ion conductivity including an ionic conductivity of $4.40~\mathrm{m}\mathrm{S}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and a ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ transfer number of 0.76. They were much higher than the $2.24~\mathrm{m}\mathrm{S}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and 0.50 of PE (Fig. S8), indicating the fast Li ion transportation in PE-ZIF$8@$ MXene. To explore the superiority of PE-ZIF- $8@$ MXene, the polymer electrolyte with $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene layers and ZIF-8 powder (PE-MXene-ZIF-8) was also fabricated and evaluated. As shown in Fig. S9, PE-MXene-ZIF-8 showed an ion conductivity of $2.48~\mathrm{mS}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , smaller than that of PE-ZIF- ${\boldsymbol{8}}\varpi$ MXene, but higher than that of PE. This reveals that MXene and ZIF-8 accelerate the transportation of Li ion in polymer electrolytes. Therefore, the accelerated Li ion transport in PE-ZIF- $8@$ MXene could be ascribed to the unique features of ZIF-8@MXene nanosheets, which have abundant functional groups that helped to weaken the coupling between Li ion and polymer chain, and also high specific surface area that provided plentiful ion transport channels. According to the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves (Fig. 3c), PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene and PE showed similar electrochemical stability in the voltage range of $1.2\mathrm{V}$ to $4.5\:\mathrm{V}$ , which could match commonly used anode materials. Thermal stability and flame-retardant mechanism performances of the polymer electrolytes were key factors for the safety of the batteries. As revealed in Fig. 3d, PE-ZIF-8@MXene exhibited a good tensile strength of 3.77 MPa, which increased by $41\%$ than $2.67~\mathrm{MPa}$ of PE. This is due to the formed hydrogen bonds between ZIF-8@MXene and the polymer base. According to the optical pictures (Fig. 3e), PE-ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene maintained its stability after being heated at 200 or $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for one hour, while PE shrank and was frizzy. The commercial Celgard membrane melted to a mass at only $200\mathrm{~\textdegree~}$ (Fig. S11). More significantly, PE-ZIF$8@$ MXene exhibited superior flame resistance. PE-ZIF-8@MXene was flame-retardant without any smoke and fire after being burned for $10~s$ (Fig. 3f); whereas the commercial Celgard membrane was easily ignited (Fig. S12).
To evaluate the electrochemical performances of PE-ZIF$8@$ MXene, galvanostatic charge–discharge and electrochemical impedance tests were conducted. As revealed in Fig. 4a, symmetric batteries with PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene exhibited a superior cycling stability with a long life up to $2000{\mathrm{h}}$ at $0.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , which was about 13 times longer than that of PE. To further investigate the cycling processes, potential-time curves at selected cycles were concentrated. As shown in Fig. 4b, PE-ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene revealed an overpotential of about $25~\mathrm{\mV}$ at the 10th cycle, much lower than $45~\mathrm{mV}$ of PE. With continuous cycles, a stable lithium/electrolyte interface was obtained, and the PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene displayed an ultralow overpotential of only $9~\mathrm{mV}$ at the 800th cycle. This can be further confirmed by the impedance results in which PE-ZIF8@MXene exhibited a slightly changed interface resistance after 20 cycles (Fig. 4c). And, the smaller interface resistance of PEZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene than that of PE at the initial and 20th cycle demonstrated the fast interface reaction (Fig. 4d and S15). Moreover, PEZIF- $8@$ MXene exhibited smaller voltage hysteresis than PE at different current densities (Fig. 4e). According to the concentrated curves, PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ MXene had flat curves with the current density increasing from $0.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ to $2{\mathrm{\mA\cm}}^{-2}$ (Fig. 4f), indicating the stable stripping/plating processes.
To further investigate the effect of the prepared electrolytes on the lithium anode, morphologies of PE-ZIF- ${\mathbf{\delta}}\cdot8@{\mathbf{\delta}}$ MXene and PE after 50 cycles were investigated. Owing to the homogenously distributed ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ MXene nanosheets with big specific surface area and good mechanism strength, it was possible to obtain a uniform ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ flux at the lithium/electrolyte interface and suppression of lithium anode (Fig. 5a). As shown in Fig. 5b, the lithium anode of batteries with PE-ZIF-8@MXene displayed a smooth and tight surface without any dendrites. PE-ZIF- $\cdot8@$ MXene could be easily stripped and exhibited a similar surface at the initial cycle without any pores (Fig. 5c). On the contrary, PE with pores was unable to maintain a stable interface, leading to lack of uniformity in $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and lithium dendrites (Fig. 5d). Therefore, it is clearly observed that the surface of lithium anode of batteries with PE was full of loose lithium dendrites with pores and cracks (Fig. 5e). These lithium dendrites grew along pores of PE, leading to the short circle of batteries. As revealed in Fig. 5f, pores of PE were filled, which made the lithium anode and electrolyte adhere to each other and were hard to separate. These results further demonstrate that PEZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene is efficient in stabilizing the lithium anodes.
Batteries with $\mathsf{L i F e P O}_{4}$ cathodes, Li anodes and PE-ZIF$8@$ MXene and PE were also assembled to further demonstrate the electrochemical performances of PE-ZIF- $8@$ MXene. As shown in Fig. S17, the battery with PE-ZIF-8@MXene exhibited only two peaks around 3.2 and $3.7~\mathrm{V}.$ This is similar to that of liquid electrolytes, indicating its chemical stability in full batteries. The peak current of PE-ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene was more than that of PE, further confirming that it quickly transports Li ion. Owing to the high ion conductivity, PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene exhibited good rate performances such as a capacity of $121\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a high current density of 5C. On the contrary, PE revealed lower capacities at any current rates than PEZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}$ MXene, especially at high current densities (Fig. 6a). The voltage-capacity curves of PE-ZIF- $\cdot8@$ MXene showed slightly increased polarization with the current density increasing from
0.5C to 2C (Fig. 6b). This was smaller than that of PE at any rates (Fig. S18) and the stable lithium/electrolyte interface of PE-ZIF$8@$ MXene. Furthermore, during the long cycling test, compared to PE, PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ MXene had a higher capacity at the beginning and a smaller capacity, which faded after 500 cycles. It presented a capacity retention of $89.6\%$ after 500 cycles (Fig. 6c). When the current density increased to 10C (Fig. S19), a long capacity growth process with a big capacity increase was generated at the beginning of the long-term stability test for the battery with PE. This was also visible at the beginning of the rate test. A short capacity growth process occurred only at 10C for the battery with PE-ZIF${8\textcircled{a}}$ MXene. This can be attributed to the stable interface between lithium and PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ MXene, which makes it to transport ion fast.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, ZIF-8@MXene nanosheets were developed through an in-situ growth process, and a polymer composite electrolyte (PE-ZIF-8@MXene) was fabricated. In this electrolyte, ZIF$8@$ MXene nanosheets with abundant functional groups and Lewis acid sites chemically bonded with the polymer base. This accelerated its ability to transport the Li ion and also enhanced its tensile strength. The instinct stability of ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@$ MXene nanosheets improved the thermostability and flame resistance of the electrolyte. Therefore, this unique PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene exhibited good performances including high ionic conductivity of $4.4~\mathrm{m}S~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , big Li ion transfer number of 0.76, excellent thermostability at $300\mathrm{~\textdegree~}$ and good flame resistance. The assembled symmetric lithium battery with PE-ZIF- $8@$ MXene exhibited a long cycling life up to $2000\mathrm{h}$ at $0.5\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and the Li|PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene|Li batteries with PE-ZIF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot8@$ MXene presented a high capacity retention of $89.6\%$ after 500 cycles. These results demonstrate the potential of MXene/MOFs nanosheets composite polymer electrolytes for next-generation lithium-based batteries.
